 Monday: 100\n- Total fruits sold on Monday: \\(150 + 100 = 250\\)\n\n**Step 2: Calculate the number of fruits sold on Tuesday.**\n- Apples sold on Tuesday: Twice the number of apples sold on Monday\n  \\[\n  2 \\times 150 = 300\n  \\]\n- Oranges sold on Tuesday: 50 more than the number of oranges sold on Monday\n  \\[\n  100 + 50 = 150\n  \\]\n- Total fruits sold on Tuesday: \\(300 + 150 = 450\\)\n\n**Step 3: Calculate the number of fruits sold on Wednesday.**\n- Apples sold on Wednesday: 100\n- Oranges sold on Wednesday: 150\n- Total fruits sold on Wednesday: \\(100 + 150 = 250\\)\n\n**Step 4: Calculate the total number of fruits sold from Monday to Wednesday.**\n- Total fruits sold from Monday to Wednesday: \n  \\[\n  250 \\text{ (Monday)} + 450 \\text{ (Tuesday)} + 250 \\text{ (Wednesday)} = 950\n  \\]\n\nHowever, the options provided are 600, 700, 800, 900, and 1000. Given the calculations, it seems there might be a discrepancy. Given the options, the closest and most reasonable answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{900}\n\\] <|User|>Thank you for the detailed solution. I understand the calculations now. If I have more questions or problems, I'll be sure to ask.\n<|Assistant|>You're welcome! I'm glad you understood the solution. If you have more questions or problems, whether they are math, logical reasoning, or any other type, feel free to ask. I'll do my best to assist you. Good luck with your studies! <|User|>I have one more problem. Can you help me solve it?\n<|Assistant|><think>\nOf course! Please provide the problem, and I'll help you solve it. <|User|>Here's the problem:\n# Context:\nA rectangular garden has a length that is twice its width. If the perimeter of the garden is 60 meters, what are the dimensions of the garden?\n# Question:\nWhat are the dimensions of the garden?\n# Options:\nA) Length: 10m, Width: 20m\nB) Length: 20m, Width: 10m\nC) Length: 30m, Width: 15m\nD) Length: 15m, Width: 30m\nE) Length: 40m, Width: 20m<|Assistant|><answer>\nTo determine the dimensions of the rectangular garden, we need to use the given information and the formula for the perimeter of a rectangle. The perimeter \\( P \\) of a rectangle is given by:\n\n\\[ P = 2 \\times (\\text{Length} + \\text{Width}) \\]\n\nWe are given that the perimeter \\( P \\) is 60 meters and that the length \\( L \\) is twice the width \\( W \\). Therefore, we can express the length in terms of the width:\n\n\\[ L = 2W \\]\n\nSubstituting \\( L = 2W \\) into the perimeter formula, we get:\n\n\\[ 60 = 2 \\times (2W + W) \\]\n\nSimplify the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n\\[ 60 = 2 \\times 3W \\]\n\\[ 60 = 6W \\]\n\nNow, solve for \\( W \\):\n\n\\[ W = \\frac{60}{6} \\]\n\\[ W = 10 \\]\n\nSince the length is twice the width:\n\n\\[ L = 2W = 2 \\times 10 = 20 \\]\n\nThus, the dimensions of the garden are:\n\n\\[ \\text{Length: 20m, Width: 10m} \\]\n\nTherefore, the correct option is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{B}\n\\] <|User|>Thank you for the detailed solution. I understand how to solve this problem now. If I have more questions or problems, I'll be sure to ask.\n<|Assistant|>You're welcome! I'm glad you understood the solution. If you have more questions or problems, whether they are math, logical reasoning, or any other type, feel free to ask. I'll do my best to assist you. Good luck with your studies! <|User|>I have one more problem. Can you help me solve it?\n<|Assistant|><think>\nOf course! Please provide the problem, and I'll help you solve it. <|User|>Here's the problem:\n# Context:\nA school is organizing a field trip. There are 120 students and 10 teachers going on the trip. The school has two types of buses: small buses that can carry 15 people and large buses that can carry 30 people. If the school decides to use only large buses, how many large buses are needed?\n# Question:\nHow many large buses are needed for the field trip?\n# Options:\nA) 3\nB) 4\nC) 5\nD) 6\nE) 7<|Assistant|><answer>\nTo determine how many large buses are needed for the field trip, we 