rlie is blue (point 10), Charlie is green (point 12).\n\n- **Blue, green people are white.**\n  - Since Charlie is blue and green (points 10 and 12), Charlie is white (point 13).\n\n- **If Bob is smart and Bob is blue then Bob is not green.**\n  - Since Bob is green (point 1), this condition does not apply.\n\nFrom the above analysis, we can conclude that:\n- Bob is green.\n- Bob is smart.\n- Bob is not white (since he is green).\n- Bob is not blue (since he is green).\n- Bob is not nice (since he is green).\n\nHowever, we need to consider the additional information:\n- **If Charlie is white then Charlie is nice.**\n  - Since Charlie is white (point 4), this condition is satisfied, and thus Charlie is nice (point 2).\n\nGiven that Bob is green and the only information we have about Bob's color is that he is green, we cannot definitively conclude that Bob is not nice. The statement \"Bob is nice\" is not contradicted by the given information, and there is no information that explicitly states Bob is not nice.\n\nTherefore, the statement \"Bob is nice\" is unknown based on the given information.\n\nThe correct answer is:.\n<answer>B) Unknown.\n</answer>\n</think>\n</answer>",
